534:
(a) There is hereby adopted by the city council of the City of Fairburn, Georgia, the most current editions, as adopted and amended from time to time by the Georgia Department of Insurance and Safety Fire Commissioner, of the following codes, standards, rules, and regulations:
(1) The International Fire Code ("IFC"), as published by the International Code Council;
(2) The National Fire Protection Association ("NFPA") standards, including, but not limited to, National Fire Protection Association standards adopted by the State of Georgia;
(3) The Life Safety Code (NFPA 101);
(4) The Rules and Regulations of the Safety Fire Commissioner, including Chapter 120-3-3;
(5) The Rules and Regulations for State Minimum Fire Safety Standards;
(6) Title 25, Fire Protection and Safety, Official Code of Georgia Annotated; and
(7) Any amendments, revisions, or successor provisions thereto as may be adopted by the State of Georgia or the city council of the City of Fairburn.
Collectively, these codes, standards, rules, and regulations shall be known and may be cited as the Fire Prevention Code of the City of Fairburn.
(b) In the event of a conflict between the provisions of the Fire Prevention Code of the City of Fairburn, the Rules and Regulations of the Safety Fire Commissioner, or any applicable state law, the more restrictive provision shall govern.
(c) Any other provision of the Code of Ordinances of the City of Fairburn that is more restrictive than the provisions of this section shall govern and control.
(d) It is the intent of this section that the City of Fairburn shall automatically enforce the latest editions of the International Fire Code and all applicable National Fire Protection Association standards as adopted by the State of Georgia, together with any amendments thereto, without the necessity of further amendment to this Code, unless otherwise directed by the city council.
(e) References in this Code or any city ordinance to specific editions of the International Fire Code, National Fire Protection Association standards, or other fire safety regulations shall be construed to mean the most current edition adopted by the State of Georgia, including all amendments thereto.
(Ord. No. 2014-22, § 1(Exh. A), 10-27-2014; Ord. of 2-4-2020(1); Ord. No. 2026-03, § 1, 5-11-2026)

580:
Each person employed as a police officer shall meet the following requirements.
(1) Be at least 20 years of age;
(2) Be a citizen of the United States;
(3) Have a high school diploma or its recognized equivalent;
(4) Not have been convicted by any state or by the federal government of any crime the punishment for which could have been imprisonment in the federal or state prison or institution nor have been convicted or sufficient misdemeanors to establish a pattern of disregard for the law; provided that, for purposes of this subsection, violations of traffic laws and other offenses involving the operation of motor vehicles when the applicant has received a pardon shall not be considered;
(5) Be fingerprinted and a search made of local, state and national fingerprint files to disclose any criminal record;
(6) Possess good moral character as determined by investigation under procedure established by the council;
(7) Have an oral interview with the hiring authority or its representative to determine the applicant's appearance, background, and ability to communicate;
(8) Be found, after examination by a licensed physician or surgeon, to be free from any physical, emotional, or mental conditions which might adversely affect his exercising the powers or duties of a peace officer; and
(9) Within six months after appointment, attend and pass a course at the state police academy to be a certified police officer.
(Ord. No. 2009-8, § 8B, 8-28-2009)

596:
The regulations shall not apply to temporary structures. No manufactured or mobile home shall be permitted as any dwelling, accessory dwelling, principal building, or structure in any residential zoning district, except in the agricultural zoning district subject to a use permit and the following regulations:
(1) Exterior siding materials, excluding gables, soffits and minor trim, for the front of every dwelling shall be 100 percent brick siding; each side shall be at least 40 percent brick, stone or cementitious siding;
(2) Vinyl siding and metal siding are prohibited;
(3) All buildings must have a permanent foundation, defined as a continuous perimeter base on which the building rests, to be constructed from masonry brick on a footing to be placed a minimum of 42 inches below the final ground level;
(4) No modular commercial buildings shall be permitted in any zoning district except the M-2 heavy industrial zoning district subject to a use permit and the following regulations:
a. Exterior siding materials, excluding gables, soffits and minor trim, for the front of every building shall be 100 percent brick, stone or cementitious siding; each side shall be at least 40 percent brick, stone or cementitious siding; vinyl siding and metal siding is prohibited.
b. All proposed developments must adhere to the design standards in the Highway 74 Overlay District.
c. All buildings must have a permanent foundation, defined as a continuous perimeter base on which the building rests, to be constructed from masonry brick on a footing to be placed a minimum of 42 inches below the final ground level.
(Ord. No. 2024-04, § 1(Exh. A), 1-22-2024)
