669:
No licensee under this article, or agent, employee or representative of such licensee shall:
(1) Cheat or defraud any person in the city;
(2) Make any false representations to any persons in the city;
(3) Barter, sell or peddle any goods, wares or merchandise other than those specified in his application for a license;
(4) Make or cause to be made, permit or allow, while upon the streets, sidewalks and public places, any excessive noise of any kind by crying, calling, shouting, or by means of any whistle, rattle, bell, horn or similar device, for the purpose of advertising or of attracting the attention or inviting the patronage of any person.

674:
(a) Permit required. No person shall engage in business as a canvasser or solicitor, calling at residences in the city without the previous consent of the occupant for the purpose of soliciting orders, sales, subscriptions or business of any kind or seeking information without first having obtained a permit therefor from the city clerk.
(b) Application. Applications for permits to solicit for the sale of goods, merchandise or rendition of services or business of any kind shall be in writing on a form provided by the city clerk and shall contain, but not be limited to the following information:
(1) The name, address and headquarters of the person applying for the permit;
(2) The name, address and headquarters of the person he represents;
(3) The nature of the products or services in which he is interested;
(4) The names of the manufacturers of such products or of the organization he represents;
(5) The name and address of the person who will be in direct charge of conducting the solicitations in the city;
(6) The dates such solicitation will be conducted;
(7) The proposed method of operation within the city;
(8) Retailers' occupation tax registration number and such other information as may be deemed necessary by the city clerk;
(9) Complete identification of the applicant;
(10) The person making such application shall swear or affirm before a notary public as to the truthfulness of the information contained in such application.
(c) Fee. The license fee to be paid by a canvasser or solicitor for profit shall be the amount established by the city council. No license shall be issued pursuant to this article until the license fee is paid.
(d) Qualifications; investigation. No permit hereunder shall be issued until five days after the application is made to the city clerk. Upon receipt of such application, the city clerk shall refer the same to the chief of police, who shall cause an investigation of the applicant's criminal record, if any. If the applicant has been convicted of a felony, then, and in that event, no permit shall be issued; if the applicant has not been convicted of any felony, then the permit hereunder shall be issued at the expiration of five days after the application is made.
(e) License to be carried. Each person shall, at all times, while soliciting or canvassing within the city, carry upon his person the permit, and the same shall be exhibited by said person whenever he is requested to do so by any officer or resident of the city.

675:
(a) Permit required. No person shall solicit contributions within the city to any charity or community project, without having first obtained a permit therefor; provided, however, no permit need be obtained by any church or organization to conduct a solicitation solely among its own membership.
(b) Application. Applications for permits to solicit funds shall be made in writing to the city clerk on forms provided by him. Such applications shall contain the following information, or in lieu thereof, a statement as to why such information cannot be furnished:
(1) The name and address of the charity or organization applying for the permit;
(2) The names and addresses of the residents of the city in charge of organizing and supervising the solicitation;
(3) The name and address of any person who is to receive a percentage of the funds collected for organizing or promoting the solicitation;
(4) The amount or percentage to be paid to such promoter or organizer;
(5) The purpose for which the collected funds are to be used;
(6) The name and address of the person or organization to which the funds are to be remitted when collected;
(7) The method to be used in conducting the solicitation;
(8) The time when such solicitation is to be made, giving dates preferred for beginning and ending the solicitation;
(9) The amount of money collected by such solicitation in the last preceding year;
(10) A statement to the effect that if a permit is granted, it will not be used or represented in any way as an endorsement by the city or any department or officer thereof;
(11) Such other information as may be deemed necessary by the city clerk.
(c) Verification. The person making such application shall swear or affirm before a notary public as to the truthfulness of the information contained in said application.
(d) Fee. The license fee to be paid by charitable or communal solicitors or canvassers shall be in the amount established by the city council. No license shall be issued pursuant to this article until the license fee is paid.
(e) Issuance. The city clerk shall cause the solicitation permit to be issued after finding:
(1) That the statements in the application are true;
(2) That control and supervision of the solicitation will be under responsible and reliable persons;
(3) That the solicitation is solely for the purpose of financing the charitable cause described, except for the attendant expense relative thereto in the application;
(4) That the license fee has been paid.

737:
(a) All transportation projects in the city shall create complete streets that allow safe and convenient travel along and across streets for users of all ages and abilities and for all modes of transportation, including motorists, bicyclists, public transportation vehicles and their passengers, and pedestrians, rail, and truck freight.
(b) Except as may otherwise be provided below, subsection (a) shall apply to all transportation projects in the City of Fairburn. This shall include new construction, reconstruction, restoration, and rehabilitation of highways, roads, and streets.
(c) The City of Fairburn Department of Community Development shall develop and implement procedures, documents, training programs, and performance measures within one year of the effective date of the ordinance from which this article is derived to ensure that the needs of all users of the highways, roads, and streets in the City of Fairburn are substantively considered during all phases of all transportation projects, including funding, planning, designing, operating, and maintaining transportation infrastructure.
(d) The City of Fairburn Department of Community Development shall develop and/or adopt design manuals, standards, and guidelines based on the latest and best practices of street design, construction, operations, and maintenance as these apply to bicycle, pedestrian, transit, and highway facilities. All manuals, standards, and guidelines must be made publicly available online. Recommended design guidelines include, but are not limited to:
• A Policy on Geometric Design of Highways and Streets (American Association of State Highway and Transportation Officials);
• Guide for the Development of Bicycle Facilities (American Association of State Highway and Transportation Officials);
• Guide for the Planning, Design, and Operation of Pedestrian Facilities (American Association of State Highway and Transportation Officials);
• Separated Bike Lane Planning and Design Guide (Federal Highway Administration);
• Urban Bikeway Design Guide (National Association of City Transportation Officials);
• Creating Walkable Communities (Federal Highway Administration Office of Safety);
• Pedestrian & Streetscape Guide (Georgia Department of Transportation).
(Ord. No. 2016-12, § 1, 11-14-2016)

